`)}}}},RRe=["BS-OP-5"],HRe=["Quality management: control, assurance and improvement","Inspection and sampling in quality control","Quality standards and external certification","Continuous improvement and total quality management"],jRe=["Quality control","Quality assurance","Quality improvement","Flexibility"],cR=[{stem:"A chocolate maker weighs and inspects a sample of bars from every batch. Any batch in which the sample bars are underweight or misshapen is rejected before it leaves the factory.",reason:"Quality control uses inspection and measurement to check that output meets set standards, and rejects output that does not. Weighing and inspecting samples, then rejecting faulty batches, is quality control.",whyNot:"Quality assurance would involve a system of standards checked by an outside body, and quality improvement would involve ongoing changes to the process itself. The factory is also not changing what it produces to meet customer demand, so flexibility does not fit."},{stem:"A manufacturer of plumbing fittings gains certification to an international quality management standard. Each year an external auditor checks that its procedures still meet the standard.",reason:"Quality assurance is a system that makes sure agreed quality standards are met throughout production, often shown by certification from an external body such as ISO. Certification plus a yearly external audit is quality assurance.",whyNot:"Quality control focuses on inspecting the output itself rather than the whole system. The business is not making continual changes to its processes, so it is not quality improvement, and nothing suggests it is adapting its product range, which rules out flexibility."},{stem:"A hospital laundry holds a short meeting each month where workers suggest small changes to their procedures. The best ideas are trialled and adopted, and turnaround times have fallen a little every quarter.",reason:"Quality improvement is an ongoing commitment to making operations more efficient and effective, usually through many small changes suggested by employees (continuous improvement). Regular staff suggestions that steadily cut turnaround times are quality improvement.",whyNot:"Nothing is being inspected and rejected, which rules out quality control, and no external standard or audit is involved, which rules out quality assurance. The laundry is getting better at the same service rather than switching between different outputs, so this is not flexibility."},{stem:"A clothing factory buys programmable sewing machines and trains staff in several tasks. It can now switch from making school uniforms to hospital scrubs within a few hours when orders change.",reason:"Flexibility is the operations performance objective of adapting quickly to changes in the type or volume of output customers want. Switching between uniforms and scrubs within hours is flexibility.",whyNot:"The change is about responding to different orders, not about inspecting output, meeting a certified standard or making ongoing small improvements to one process. So none of the three quality management approaches is the main focus."}],FRe={id:"business_studies/2025/q08",version:1,variants:cR.length,syllabusTopics:[...RRe],learningTopics:HRe,generate(t){const e=cR[t];return{question:[e.stem,"","Which operations concept does this best illustrate?"].join(`
