`)],sampleAnswer:["Each ball starts with purely horizontal velocity. Its horizontal motion has constant speed, while its vertical motion starts from rest with acceleration $g$ downwards. Measure time from when each ball leaves its own ledge. The delay between the two launches then does not matter, because each flight is considered on its own.","","Let $OQ = h$, so $OP = kh$.","","**Ball from $P$.** Height above ground: $y = kh - \\tfrac{1}{2}gt^2$. It lands when $y = 0$:","","$$t_P = \\sqrt{\\frac{2kh}{g}}, \\qquad \\text{horizontal distance } = U t_P = U\\sqrt{\\frac{2kh}{g}}$$","","**Ball from $Q$.** In the same way:","","$$t_Q = \\sqrt{\\frac{2h}{g}}, \\qquad \\text{horizontal distance } = V t_Q = V\\sqrt{\\frac{2h}{g}}$$","","**Same landing point.** Both horizontal distances are measured from $O$, so they are equal:","","$$U\\sqrt{\\frac{2kh}{g}} = V\\sqrt{\\frac{2h}{g}} \\implies U\\sqrt{k}\\sqrt{\\frac{2h}{g}} = V\\sqrt{\\frac{2h}{g}} \\implies V = U\\sqrt{k}$$","","Interpretation: the lower ball has less time in the air, by a factor of $\\sqrt{k}$, so it must be $\\sqrt{k}$ times faster to cover the same horizontal distance."].join(`
