`),sampleAnswer:["A hydrogen atom has a **nucleus** containing one **proton**, with one","**electron** outside the nucleus. To answer this using the Standard Model,","you need to identify which particles are fundamental and which are made of","smaller particles.","","**Proton structure (Standard Model):**","A proton is a **baryon**, so it is not fundamental. It is made of **two up quarks**","and **one down quark**. These quarks are held together by the **strong nuclear","force**, with **gluons** acting as the exchange particles.","- Up quark charge: $+\\tfrac{2}{3}e$ each","- Down quark charge: $-\\tfrac{1}{3}e$","- Proton charge: $+\\tfrac{2}{3} + \\tfrac{2}{3} - \\tfrac{1}{3} = +e$","","**Electron:**","The electron is a fundamental **lepton** with charge $-e$. It is not made","of quarks, so under the Standard Model it is treated as an elementary particle.","","**Force holding atom together:**","The proton and electron are bound by the **electromagnetic force** (attraction","between opposite charges $+e$ and $-e$).","","So, in summary, a hydrogen atom contains one composite particle (the proton,","made of quarks) and one fundamental particle (the electron, a lepton), and the","atom is held together by electromagnetic attraction."].join(`
