`),marks:2},{question:["The magnetic field is switched off and the bar is pushed up the slope at the same","steady speed as before.","","Explain why the force needed is now different."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["At a steady speed, the forces on the bar along the slope are balanced.","","**With the field on**, the induced current $I$ flows along the bar, which is","perpendicular to the vertical field, so the bar feels a magnetic force","$F_B = BIL$. By Lenz’s law this force opposes the motion. It is horizontal and","points back towards the connecting wire, so its component $F_B\\cos\\theta$ acts","down the slope. The push must balance this as well as the component of the","weight down the slope, $mg\\sin\\theta$, and any friction.","","**With the field off**, the flux through the circuit does not change, so no emf","is induced, no current flows and there is no magnetic force on the bar. The push","only has to balance $mg\\sin\\theta$ and friction.","","So a **smaller** force is needed without the field. The extra force needed with","the field is the mechanical work being converted into electrical energy, which is","then dissipated as heat in the circuit."].join(`
