`),marks:2},{question:"Torque also turns the coil of a simple DC motor. Explain **TWO** changes that would increase the torque on the coil.",sampleAnswer:["The torque on a rectangular coil in a uniform magnetic field is","","$$\\tau = nIAB\\sin\\theta$$","","where $n$ is the number of turns, $I$ the current, $A$ the area of the coil, $B$ the magnetic field strength and $\\theta$ the angle between the field and the normal to the coil. Increasing any of $n$, $I$, $A$ or $B$ increases the torque.","","**Change 1 — more turns on the coil.** Each turn carries the current, so each turn has its own pair of equal and opposite forces ($F = BIL\\sin\\theta$) on its two sides. With more turns, these forces add up, giving a larger total turning effect.","","**Change 2 — a larger current**, for example by increasing the supply voltage. The force on each side of the coil, $F = BIL$, is proportional to $I$, so a larger current gives larger forces and hence a larger torque.","","Other acceptable changes: using stronger magnets (larger $B$), or a coil of larger area (the sides are further from the axis, so the same force acts at a larger distance)."].join(`
